# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Lecture 44 objective pool B -- Pleural Disorders: aspiration, chest tube, pleurodesis and surgery; spontaneous
pneumothorax treatment, stability and patient education; traumatic and tension pneumothorax (slides 36-68;
slide 60 is an infographic read from the 1920 px render).

KEYS ARE WRITTEN SHORT ON PURPOSE -- detail lives in the explanation.
EXPLANATIONS follow tools/question_writing/EXPLAIN_DEEP.md (Jaxon, 2026-10-29): the key names the decisive
clue and the fact; each wrong choice says why it fails here, the replacing fact, and a tell.

Set 1 is recall against the syllabus objectives, so no stem carries a patient age. Never keyed (scope): the s58
timings and size percentages, the s60 four-day air-leak rule, chest tube sizes and drainage-system parts, the
pleurodesis "extra fluid / layers slide" slip (slide 45 keyed: adhesions obliterate the space; the slip appears
only as a wrong option), a needle-site item for a large habitus, any 4th/5th intercostal space as a wrong
needle-site option. The large primary pneumothorax item keys chest tube (s55) and offers no needle or catheter
aspiration, small-bore catheter or Heimlich valve option (s58 conflict). Tension next-step stems state that the
chest tube is not readily available, or ask what follows needle decompression (s66 vs s58).
"""
